---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Is Event Management a Good Career in India? Pay, Burnout, Verdict';
const description =
  'Is event management a good career in India? Real pay bands, event-day burnout, freelance income risk, and who this logistics-heavy path actually fits — the honest verdict.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/is-event-management-a-good-career-in-india';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-08-17';
const publishedDateLabel = '17 August 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'glamour-reality', label: 'The glamour-reality gap' },
  { id: 'pay-ceiling', label: 'The real pay bands' },
  { id: 'income-risk', label: 'Why income is unstable, not just low' },
  { id: 'seasonality', label: 'The seasonal demand problem' },
  { id: 'scaling', label: 'How the biggest earners actually scale' },
  { id: 'ai-risk', label: 'Will AI replace event management jobs' },
  { id: 'who-fits', label: 'Who this genuinely fits' },
  { id: 'who-should-not', label: 'Who should not choose this path' },
  { id: 'alternatives', label: 'Genuinely better alternatives, and when they are not' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'The 4-Checkpoint Protocol' },
  { id: 'gates', label: 'The 3 Gates before you commit' },
  { id: 'verdict', label: 'The verdict framework' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes to avoid' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const glamourRealityComparison = {
  gold: [
    'Instagram reels of decorated stages, celebrity walk-ins, and a planner holding a clipboard next to a champagne tower are the version of this career most people see before they choose it.',
    'Course brochures and coordinator job posts lean on words like "creative," "dynamic," and "glamorous industry" far more than they mention 14-hour shifts, vendor no-shows, or chasing a client for a pending payment.',
    'The planner everyone sees is the one presenting the finished result. The planner nobody sees was awake since 5 a.m. fixing a generator failure two hours before the first guest arrived.',
  ],
  teal: [
    'The actual daily work is closer to logistics and operations than to design: vendor calls, cost sheets, floor plans, permits, load-in schedules, and a dozen small sequencing decisions that decide whether the event runs on time.',
    'Event day itself is the highest-pressure, longest-hours day of the entire project — routinely 12-16 hours on your feet, managing a live situation where nothing is allowed to go visibly wrong.',
    'Client management is emotionally taxing in a way most job descriptions do not capture: managing expectations, absorbing last-minute changes, and staying composed with an anxious host two hours before doors open.',
  ],
};

const payRows = [
  { path: 'Fresher coordinator, agency', pay: 'Roughly ₹2.4-4.6 lakh/year', notes: 'Trainee-level, learning showflows and vendor coordination under someone else\'s ownership. Overtime around event days is common and often unpaid at this stage.' },
  { path: 'Mid-level planner, agency', pay: 'Roughly ₹5-8 lakh/year', notes: 'Full project ownership, direct client contact, and a small team reporting in. This is the level where specialising starts to matter for further pay growth.' },
  { path: 'Freelance day rate, early career', pay: 'Roughly ₹5,000-20,000 per event day', notes: 'Paid per project, not monthly. A slow month with no bookings pays nothing, regardless of how strong the last event went.' },
  { path: 'Freelance day rate, established metro planner', pay: 'Roughly ₹30,000-50,000 per event day', notes: 'Reached after several years of a proven client base and reputation, concentrated in Mumbai, Delhi NCR, and Bangalore.' },
  { path: 'Wedding planner, established', pay: 'Roughly ₹6-15 lakh/year', notes: 'Usually billed as 10-20% of the total event budget across 20-30 weddings a year, not a flat salary — so total income still tracks how many bookings land in a short season.' },
  { path: 'Senior manager or agency owner', pay: 'Roughly ₹10-20 lakh/year+', notes: 'Strategic oversight, P&L ownership, and large-scale event delivery. Agency owners carry business risk (staff pay, vendor advances, unpaid client invoices) on top of the ceiling.' },
];

const incomeRiskItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Freelancers and junior agency staff absorb the volatility first',
    body: 'A salaried mid-level planner has a floor: a fixed monthly paycheck regardless of how many events ran that month. A freelancer or an agency junior paid partly on project bonuses does not have that floor — a thin month is a thin paycheck, full stop.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Payment terms in this industry are genuinely client-favoured',
    body: 'Clients frequently pay a deposit upfront and settle the balance only after the event, sometimes weeks late. A freelancer who fronts vendor advances out of pocket and waits on a delayed final payment is carrying real cash-flow risk on every single project.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Annual income can look healthy while monthly income is genuinely unstable',
    body: 'An established wedding planner earning ₹10 lakh a year sounds stable on paper. In practice, that income can land almost entirely across a five- or six-month wedding window, leaving long stretches with little or no cash coming in — a very different experience from a fixed monthly salary of the same annual total.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'New freelancers routinely underprice to win their first clients',
    body: 'It is common to quote too low out of fear of losing a first booking, then struggle to raise rates with the same client base later. The stronger move is pricing fairly from the start and treating a few early, lower-paying events purely as portfolio-building, not as the new normal rate.',
  },
];

const seasonalityRows = [
  { segment: 'Weddings and social events', pattern: 'Heavily clustered around a few months a year depending on the region, with long off-season gaps.', risk: 'High — the segment most people picture first is also the most seasonally concentrated.' },
  { segment: 'Corporate events and conferences', pattern: 'Runs more evenly across the year but still dips around major holiday periods and slow business quarters.', risk: 'Moderate — steadier, but not immune to slow stretches.' },
  { segment: 'Exhibitions and trade shows', pattern: 'Tied to fixed industry calendar dates, often booked well in advance.', risk: 'Moderate — predictable timing, but volume depends on which trade calendars a planner has access to.' },
  { segment: 'Concerts and entertainment events', pattern: 'Clusters around touring seasons and festival calendars.', risk: 'Moderate to high, depending on how diversified the client roster is.' },
];

const scalingCards = [
  {
    title: 'Specialise instead of staying a generalist',
    body: 'Wedding planning, corporate events, and experiential marketing reward genuinely different skills and different client relationships. Planners who pick one segment deliberately and build a reputation in it out-earn generalists who stay spread thin across all of them.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Move from freelance execution into agency or brand ownership',
    body: 'The real ceiling in this field belongs to people who eventually own the business relationship — running an agency, owning a niche (destination weddings, corporate experiential, festival production) — rather than only executing someone else\'s brief project by project.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Build a repeat and referral client base, not one-off bookings',
    body: 'The planners with the steadiest income are not the ones chasing new leads every month. They are the ones with corporate retainer clients, repeat family bookings, or vendor and venue partnerships that keep sending qualified referrals without a fresh sales cycle each time.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Add a second monetisable skill: sales, vendor negotiation, or data',
    body: 'Planners who can also run cost negotiation at scale, sponsorship or vendor-partnership deals, or basic budget analytics become genuinely more valuable to mid-size clients who cannot afford a specialist for every function.',
  },
];

const aiComparison = {
  gold: [
    'Guest list management, RSVP tracking, and basic scheduling — spreadsheet and app-based tools already automate most of this, and AI-assisted planning platforms are only extending that further.',
    'First-pass vendor discovery and price comparison, where AI tools can now surface options and starting quotes faster than manually calling a dozen vendors.',
    'Budget templates, cost-sheet drafts, and run-of-show document drafting, which AI tools can generate a usable first version of in minutes.',
  ],
  teal: [
    'Vendor negotiation and relationship management, where trust, leverage, and reading a person face-to-face still decide the final price and the favour called in during a crisis.',
    'Client emotional management — reassuring an anxious host, managing a family disagreement over the guest list, delivering bad news about a budget overrun without losing the client.',
    'Live, on-ground crisis handling on event day itself: a vendor no-show, a weather shift, a VIP change, or any of the dozen things that go wrong that no scripted system can fully anticipate.',
  ],
};

const aiStagedPath = [
  { num: '1', title: 'Awareness', body: 'Know which parts of your workload are already automatable — RSVP tracking, first-draft budgets, basic vendor sourcing — so you are not spending billable hours on tasks a tool can do faster.' },
  { num: '2', title: 'Assisted execution', body: 'Let AI-assisted planning tools handle the first draft of a cost sheet, timeline, or run-of-show document, then spend your time refining it against the specific client and venue.' },
  { num: '3', title: 'Quality control', body: 'Build the habit of checking any AI-suggested vendor quote or timeline before it reaches a client — a wrong number or an unrealistic load-in window costs trust fast in this business.' },
  { num: '4', title: 'Workflow design', body: 'Redesign how your team\'s pre-event workload is split once routine documentation is automated, so your strongest people spend more time on vendor relationships and client management, not paperwork.' },
  { num: '5', title: 'Specialisation', body: 'Pair strong on-ground execution with a commercial layer — sponsorship deals, vendor-partnership negotiation, or a niche like experiential marketing — which is where the field\'s clearer pay growth currently sits.' },
];

const whoFitsItems = [
  { label: 'Genuine fit', title: 'You stay calm and decisive when something breaks in real time', body: 'A vendor running late, a power outage, or a guest list mix-up is a normal Tuesday in this field, not a rare disaster. If you can make a fast call under pressure without spiralling, that composure is the single most valuable trait here.' },
  { label: 'Genuine fit', title: 'You are genuinely detail-obsessed, not just organised on paper', body: 'The work is built from a hundred small, unglamorous details done right: a correctly labelled vendor invoice, a load-in time confirmed twice, a seating chart double-checked. People who find that satisfying, not tedious, last longer here.' },
  { label: 'Genuine fit', title: 'Working odd hours and weekends does not quietly wear you down', body: 'Event days land on weekends, evenings, and festival periods by default, not by exception. If that rhythm genuinely fits your life rather than fighting it, the early years are far more sustainable.' },
];

const whoShouldNotRows = [
  { signal: 'You are drawn to this mainly because it "sounds fun" or looks good on social media', reality: 'The daily work is logistics, spreadsheets, and vendor calls. The fun, photographed moment is a tiny fraction of the actual job.' },
  { signal: 'You need predictable income every single month to cover fixed obligations', reality: 'Freelance and early-agency pay in this field is genuinely lumpy, tied to how many events land in a given stretch, not a steady monthly figure.' },
  { signal: 'High-pressure, high-stakes real-time problem-solving genuinely drains you rather than energising you', reality: 'Event day is the entire job compressed into 12-16 hours where visible mistakes are not an option. This is not optional stress you can plan around — it is the core of the work.' },
  { signal: 'You want a fixed weekday schedule with weekends consistently off', reality: 'Weekends and festivals are peak workload here, not personal time, for as long as you stay in guest- and client-facing execution.' },
];

const alternativeCards = [
  { badge: 'If steady monthly income is the real priority', title: 'Corporate operations or project management roles', body: 'If the honest pull is the logistics-and-coordination skill set itself, not client-facing service under public pressure, corporate operations or project management roles use similar organisational strengths on a fixed salary with far less event-day volatility.' },
  { badge: 'If it is guest-facing service you want, on a different schedule', title: 'Hotel management and hospitality operations', body: 'Hospitality shares the guest-service and vendor-coordination skill set but runs on a property-based shift roster rather than a project-based event calendar — a genuinely different trade-off, with its own shift-heavy demands. The honest read on that path lives in is hotel management a good career.' },
  { badge: 'If the draw is the live-event energy without full ownership risk', title: 'In-house event coordination at a company or venue', body: 'Working as an in-house events or experiences coordinator for a company, venue, or hotel group keeps the live-event work but usually comes with a fixed salary and less exposure to the freelance income swings that hit independent planners hardest.' },
  { badge: 'When event management is genuinely the better call', title: 'When building and owning client relationships is the actual draw', body: 'If what you actually want is to build a business around your own reputation and client relationships, with a real path to specialising in a high-value niche like corporate experiential or destination weddings, few adjacent fields offer that ownership path as directly as this one does.' },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Biology / work style',
    body: 'Can you handle 12-16 hour event days on your feet, irregular weekend and evening hours, and staying composed through a live crisis — or does your energy genuinely need a fixed, predictable daily schedule?',
    note: 'Stress tolerance under time pressure predicts who lasts in this field longer than creativity or "loving events" does.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Can your finances absorb a genuinely modest and irregular starting income (roughly ₹2.4-4.6 lakh a year as an employed fresher, or lumpy freelance income) for the first couple of years while you build a client base and reputation? Or does your situation need steadier monthly income sooner?',
    note: 'Treat roughly 10% of your total education budget as the default ceiling for a diploma or certification fee itself, keeping the rest for the lean early-income years and any unpaid internship stretch.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'India\'s event industry keeps adding real volume across weddings, corporate work, and experiential marketing, but entry-level coordinator roles are crowded and starting pay stays flat without deliberate specialisation. Is your target segment — weddings, corporate, exhibitions, or experiential — one where you can realistically build a distinct reputation?',
    note: 'Overall sector growth and a crowded, flat-paying entry tier are both true at once. They describe different layers of the same industry.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival / differentiation',
    body: 'A general event management certificate from a mid-tier institute is close to a commodity credential now. Real placement or internship proof, a documented event you actually ran, and one specialisation are what separate you from a large batch of similarly credentialed graduates.',
    note: 'The real question is not "will event management give me a job." It is "what specific, provable event have I run that makes a client or employer pick me over the next planner who looks the same on paper."',
  },
];

const gateItems = [
  { num: 'Gate 1', title: 'Proof of skill', body: 'One real, documented piece of event work — an event you volunteered at, assisted on, or ran yourself, start to finish, even a small one. Not a course-completion certificate.' },
  { num: 'Gate 2', title: 'Proof of communication', body: 'Explain that piece of work in under two minutes to someone with zero events background, ending with what actually went wrong and how you handled it.' },
  { num: 'Gate 3', title: 'Proof of value', body: 'Show it to one working event professional, not an admissions counsellor, and ask honestly whether it reflects what the job actually pays for and demands day to day. Let this take as long as it genuinely needs — one honest conversation is enough for some people, others need a shadowed event day first.' },
];

const verdictYes = [
  'You genuinely enjoy being the person who solves a live logistics problem under time pressure, not just the idea of "working in events."',
  'You are realistic about modest, irregular early-career pay and are willing to build a client base or cross-segment range before expecting steadier income.',
  'You can accept event-day hours, weekend and festival work, and real physical and emotional stamina demands as a trade-off you weighed, not a surprise.',
  'You are willing to specialise — a segment, a niche, or a second monetisable skill like vendor negotiation or sponsorship deals — instead of staying a generalist coordinator indefinitely.',
];

const verdictNo = [
  'You are choosing this mainly for the social-media image or a vague "I love planning parties" feeling, without checking the logistics-heavy daily reality.',
  'You need predictable monthly income and cannot absorb the cash-flow gaps that come with freelance or early-agency pay in this field.',
  'High-pressure, real-time problem-solving under public scrutiny genuinely drains you rather than something you find satisfying to handle.',
  'You are assuming a certificate or diploma alone gets you hired, rather than something you would have to prove with real event work.',
];

const mistakeItems = [
  { num: '01', title: 'Choosing an institute mainly on brochure glamour instead of placement reality', body: 'Ask any shortlisted institute for their last two years of actual placement partners and stipend ranges, and weigh the fee against the 10%-of-education-budget planning check before you sign — not the campus tour.' },
  { num: '02', title: 'Never talking to a working event professional before committing time and fees', body: 'Course marketing and Instagram describe a very different version of this career than the entry-level reality. One honest conversation with someone actually running events reveals more than another month of watching brochure videos.' },
  { num: '03', title: 'Assuming the income instability "will not apply to me"', body: 'Test your tolerance for lumpy, project-based income before going independent — track how a full wedding season versus a slow month would actually affect your finances, using real numbers, not optimism.' },
  { num: '04', title: 'Treating every event segment as interchangeable', body: 'Wedding clients, corporate procurement teams, and concert production crews reward different temperaments and different vendor networks. Pick a segment deliberately once you have tried more than one, instead of drifting into whichever internship you found first.' },
  { num: '05', title: 'Underpricing early freelance work out of fear of losing the first client', body: 'New freelancers who quote too low to win early bookings often struggle to raise rates with the same client base later. Price fairly from the start, and treat a few early low-paying events purely as portfolio-building.' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is event management a good career in India?',
    a: 'Yes, for people who genuinely enjoy live problem-solving under pressure and are realistic about the trade-off: modest, often irregular early-career pay and physically and emotionally demanding event days, in exchange for a real path toward stronger income once you specialise or build your own client base. It is a weak choice for someone drawn mainly to the social-media image of the job, or who needs predictable monthly income from day one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is event management a stressful career?',
    a: 'Yes, particularly around event days themselves, which routinely run 12-16 hours with real-time problems that need an immediate decision — a vendor no-show, a schedule slip, a client changing their mind hours before doors open. It becomes more manageable once someone builds enough experience and team support to delegate parts of that pressure, but the core job never fully removes it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does an event manager earn in India?',
    a: 'Fresher coordinators at agencies typically start around ₹2.4-4.6 lakh a year. Mid-level planners with full project ownership earn roughly ₹5-8 lakh a year. Freelance day rates run from about ₹5,000-20,000 per event early on, up to ₹30,000-50,000 for established metro-city planners. Established wedding planners handling 20-30 weddings a year often earn ₹6-15 lakh annually, usually billed as a percentage of the event budget rather than a flat salary. Senior managers and agency owners can earn ₹10-20 lakh a year or more, alongside real business risk.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is event management income stable?',
    a: 'Not for most freelancers and junior agency staff. Payment terms often favour the client, with balances settled only after the event, sometimes late. Income also clusters heavily around wedding season and peak corporate quarters, so an annual total that looks healthy can still mean several months with little or no cash coming in. Salaried mid-level and senior roles at agencies are steadier, since they carry a fixed monthly paycheck regardless of how many events ran that month.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will AI replace event management jobs?',
    a: 'Not the career as a whole, but AI is already automating a real share of the routine work — RSVP tracking, first-draft budgets, basic vendor sourcing, and cost-sheet templates. What still needs a human is vendor negotiation, client emotional management, and live crisis handling on event day itself. The safer response is pairing strong execution skills with a commercial layer, like sponsorship deals or vendor-partnership negotiation, rather than staying a generalist coordinator.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is event management better than hotel management as a career?',
    a: 'It depends on what is actually driving the choice. Event management is project-based with intense short bursts around each event and a real path to owning client relationships and a business. Hotel management is property-based with a rostered shift structure and a longer but steadier climb toward department-head and GM-level pay. Neither one is automatically better — compare the pay pattern, the schedule structure, and whether you want to build a client-facing business or work inside an established property system. The honest read on the hospitality side lives in is hotel management a good career.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know if event management genuinely fits me before I commit?',
    a: 'Pass three real tests before spending time and fees on it: build one documented piece of real event proof (a volunteered event, an assisted role, or a small event you ran yourself), explain it in under two minutes to someone outside the industry, and show it to one working event professional and ask honestly whether it reflects what the job actually pays for and demands. Some people need only one honest conversation and a shadowed event day to know; others need a longer trial stretch first — let it take as long as it genuinely needs.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const relatedGuides = [
  { href: '/blog/career-options/how-to-get-into-event-management-india', label: 'Event management career India: how to get into event management, segment by segment' },
  { href: '/blog/career-options/is-hotel-management-a-good-career-in-india', label: 'Is hotel management a good career in India? Pay, hours, verdict' },
  { href: '/blog/career-options/good-careers', label: 'Good careers: the 5-axis test for what actually makes one good' },
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is event management a good career in India? Yes — but only for someone who accepts the real trade-off underneath the highlight-reel photos.</strong>
    The honest split is this: event-day hours are genuinely long and pressure-heavy, and freelance or junior income is
    genuinely lumpy — but the field also has a real path toward stronger, steadier income for people who specialise
    in a segment, build a repeat client base, or move into agency ownership. Graduates who treat this as a deliberate
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> — logistics judgment, vendor negotiation, and composure under
    pressure — are the ones who actually turn event work into real <strong>high income opportunities</strong> and a
    path toward <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>. People who go in on the Instagram version alone are the
    ones most likely to burn out in the first couple of seasons.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Yes, event management is a good career for the right person — but the honest answer splits the field into a genuinely demanding, income-unstable entry tier and a real payoff for people who specialise or build a client base.</li>
      <li>Event days routinely run 12-16 hours with real-time problems that need an immediate decision. This is not occasional stress — it is the core of the job for as long as you stay in guest- and client-facing execution.</li>
      <li>Starting pay is modest (₹2.4-4.6 lakh a year as an employed fresher), and freelance income is genuinely irregular, tied to seasonal demand and client-favoured payment terms — not a steady monthly figure.</li>
      <li>Real headroom exists: established wedding planners often earn ₹6-15 lakh a year, top freelancers ₹30,000-50,000 per event day, and agency owners ₹10-20 lakh a year or more — reached through specialisation, not tenure alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Building a genuine high-value skill portfolio — segment specialisation plus a commercial layer, with visible proof of events actually run — is what turns event management into real high income opportunities, not the certificate alone.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your own fit with one real, documented event you volunteered at, assisted on, or ran yourself before committing real fees to an expensive institute.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you already know event management is the direction and want the full segment-by-segment breakdown — the five
    event types, both entry routes, and how to build proof with no company behind you — that lives in
    <a href="/blog/career-options/how-to-get-into-event-management-india">event management career India: how to get into event management, segment by segment</a>.
    This article stays one step earlier and answers the harder question underneath it: is the field actually worth
    building a career around, for you specifically, given how demanding and unpredictable the early years genuinely are.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a clearer read on whether high-pressure, logistics-heavy work genuinely fits your working style, use
    the <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career &amp; Skills Compass</a> before you commit time and real fees to
    this decision.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is event management a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Event management is a real, growing career in India, not a dressed-up creative hobby with no ceiling. But "is
    event management good" and "will the first couple of years feel good" are two different questions, and most
    articles on this topic blur them together until you cannot tell which one is actually being answered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the daily reality — long event-day hours, real-time crisis management, and genuinely
    unstable early income — is demanding and not exaggerated. The payoff on the other side — specialisation pay,
    established-planner income, and a real path to agency ownership — is also genuinely real, but it belongs to
    people who build a client base or cross-segment range and treat the early grind as skill-building, not a
    personal failure.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "events is a glamorous, creative dream career, join now" pitch flooding institute brochures and
      Instagram reels, and it is not the "never do event management, it will burn you out" warning making rounds on
      career forums either. Both are incomplete. The field genuinely rewards people who go in with accurate
      expectations and build real range — and genuinely burns out people who went in on the highlight reel alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="glamour-reality">The glamour-reality gap</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the part most "career in event management" pages soften. The version of this career shown in course
    marketing and social media, and the version a fresh coordinator actually lives through in year one, are
    describing two different jobs, and the gap between them is the real source of early burnout in this field.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the highlight reel shows</div>
      <ul>
        {glamourRealityComparison.gold.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the daily work actually is</div>
      <ul>
        {glamourRealityComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither side cancels the other out. The client relationships, the finished-event satisfaction, and the real
      income headroom in this field are genuine, and so is the logistics-heavy, high-pressure, financially unstable
      entry tier — that entry tier is exactly where most of the field's early burnout happens.
    </p>
    <p>
      Going in with accurate expectations, not brochure expectations, is what separates people who build a real
      career here from people who quit inside the first year or two.
    </p>
